WEBVTT

00:00:00.103 --> 00:00:06.103
Use estimated temperature equals six plus four times the ring index, then calculate error

00:00:06.116 --> 00:00:08.768
estimated minus observed temperature.

00:00:09.224 --> 00:00:12.467
Evaluate three years unused in calibration.

00:00:12.921 --> 00:00:17.132
8 minutes: calculate 4, compare 3, record 1.

00:00:17.592 --> 00:00:21.704
Pause the video and substitute each index into the equation.

00:00:22.158 --> 00:00:28.072
Use estimated minus observed temperature for the error, consistently across all rows.

00:00:28.526 --> 00:00:32.178
Check the units and the signs after doing the arithmetic.

00:00:32.632 --> 00:00:38.160
Resume when you have recorded your predictions and compare them with the following answer.

00:00:38.963 --> 00:00:44.859
Predictions are 9.2, 10.0 and 10.8degrees Celsius.

00:00:45.321 --> 00:00:52.123
Errors are +0.2, −0.1 and −0.1degrees Celsius.

00:00:52.584 --> 00:00:56.834
Agreement in this small example is not a general accuracy claim.

00:00:57.294 --> 00:01:02.492
The mean signed error is zero here, but the individual errors are not zero.

00:01:02.952 --> 00:01:05.577
Positive and negative errors cancel.

00:01:06.031 --> 00:01:10.807
We need to examine both average bias and the size of year-to-year errors.

00:01:11.268 --> 00:01:16.202
A model can have little average bias while still making substantial mistakes in particular

00:01:16.215 --> 00:01:17.692
years.

00:01:18.495 --> 00:01:21.233
Correlation measures shared variation.

00:01:21.695 --> 00:01:25.188
Bias is systematic over- or underestimation.

00:01:25.642 --> 00:01:30.951
RMSE is the square root of mean squared error, in degrees Celsius here.

00:01:31.405 --> 00:01:35.635
High correlation can coexist with a persistent temperature error.

00:01:36.089 --> 00:01:41.063
If every estimate is one degree too warm, the series can still rise and fall with

00:01:41.076 --> 00:01:42.550
observations.

00:01:43.010 --> 00:01:48.603
Squaring errors before averaging prevents positive and negative mistakes from cancelling.

00:01:49.063 --> 00:01:53.384
Correlation and prediction error answer different questions.

00:01:54.187 --> 00:01:58.872
Model B adds 1degrees Celsius to every estimate from model A.

00:01:59.334 --> 00:02:02.551
The pattern is unchanged, but the errors grow.

00:02:03.005 --> 00:02:06.064
Use more than correlation to assess agreement.

00:02:06.518 --> 00:02:11.485
The two model curves move in parallel because model B simply adds a constant.

00:02:11.939 --> 00:02:16.834
Adding a constant does not alter correlation, but it does change bias relative to

00:02:16.847 --> 00:02:18.360
observations.

00:02:18.821 --> 00:02:24.650
Decide whether the research question concerns the timing of variation, the actual values, or

00:02:24.663 --> 00:02:28.332
both, and choose diagnostics accordingly.

00:02:29.135 --> 00:02:33.702
Compare A, B and the constant prediction of 10degrees Celsius.

00:02:34.164 --> 00:02:40.032
RMSE = the square root of the sum of squared errors divided by the number of years.

00:02:40.493 --> 00:02:45.236
6 minutes: calculate and discuss whether correlation is sufficient.

00:02:45.690 --> 00:02:49.749
Pause and calculate squared errors for all three predictions.

00:02:50.203 --> 00:02:53.591
Model A uses the errors already calculated.

00:02:54.045 --> 00:03:00.104
Model B is one degree warmer at every point, and the baseline always predicts ten degrees.

00:03:00.558 --> 00:03:05.282
Compare the models with that simple baseline before deciding whether their extra structure

00:03:05.295 --> 00:03:06.952
is useful.

00:03:07.755 --> 00:03:21.282
RMSE: A ≈ 0.14degrees Celsius, B ≈ 1.01degrees Celsius, constant ≈ 0.78degrees Celsius.

00:03:21.744 --> 00:03:27.040
A and B both have correlation ≈ 0.996.

00:03:27.494 --> 00:03:31.382
A three-year synthetic illustration of evaluation design.

00:03:31.836 --> 00:03:37.988
In this example, model B has high correlation yet a larger error than the constant baseline.

00:03:38.441 --> 00:03:42.218
That demonstrates why correlation alone is insufficient.

00:03:42.678 --> 00:03:47.553
The numerical ranking belongs to this deliberately small synthetic dataset.

00:03:48.007 --> 00:03:53.756
It should not be treated as evidence about the performance of a real climate reconstruction.
